    {
      "who": "MIA",
      "text": "A purr is a cat's soft rumble. Scientists think tiny muscles in its throat buzz as the cat breathes in and out.",
      "board": {
        "type": "big",
        "icon": "img:Human_physiology/Servier/larynx",
        "text": "A rumble in the throat",
        "cue": "throat",
        "sub": "Buzz in, buzz out",
        "sub_cue": "breathes"
      }
    },
    {
      "who": "LEO",
      "text": "So it purrs breathing in AND breathing out?"
    },
    {
      "who": "MIA",
      "text": "Yes! That's why a purr can keep going and going, like a tiny engine.",
      "punch": "engine",
      "board": {
        "type": "big",
        "icon": "🔁",
        "text": "A non-stop purr!",
        "cue": "going",
        "sub": "Like a tiny engine",
        "sub_cue": "engine"
      }
    },
    {
      "who": "LEO",
      "text": "Do cats purr because they're happy?",
      "board": {
        "type": "big",
        "icon": "😺",
        "text": "Happy cat?",
        "cue": "happy"
      }
    },
    {
      "who": "MIA",
      "text": "Often, yes! A cat may purr when it's cozy, cuddled, or ready for a nap.",
      "board": {
        "type": "gallery",
        "title": "Happy purrs",
        "items": [
          {
            "icon": "🛋️",
